hing_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grids are made of.** Every input is a 15×15 field of random colours. One structure repeats in all of them: a frame of 5s in the top-left corner. It is the row y=3 and the column x=3, which enclose a 3×3 window at rows 0–2, columns 0–2.\n\n**What changes.** In each output, exactly 16 cells become colour 5. Together they form the outline of a 5×5 square. Its 3×3 interior is left untouched. So the task reduces to finding where that square goes.\n\n**Where the square goes.** Compare the interior of the new frame with the corner window:\n\n- **Pair 1.** Interior at rows 9–11, cols 6–8 is `3 4 0 / 0 0 4 / 0 8 3`. The top-left window rows 0–2, cols 0–2 is `3 4 0 / 0 0 4 / 0 8 3`. They are identical.\n- **Pair 2.** Interior at rows 5–7, cols 5–7 is `0 7 6 / 7 0 0 / 0 9 0`. This is the same as the top-left window.\n- **Pair 3.** Interior at rows 8–10, cols 7–9 is `4 0 2 / 0 7 0 / 0 6 6`. Again this is the same as the top-left window.\n\n**The rule.** The corner frame marks a 3×3 pattern. Find the other place in the grid where that exact 3×3 pattern appears. Then draw a 5-coloured frame around it, one cell outside the pattern on every side. Frame cells overwrite whatever was there; the interior is not touched.\n\n**Construction.**\n1. Read the 3×3 pattern from rows 0–2, columns 0–2.\n2. Scan every 3×3 window except the corner one, and take the window that matches exactly.\n3. Paint the one-cell ring around it with colour 5. If the ring would go past the grid edge, skip those cells.\n4. Emit the result.\n\nThe program follows these steps for the test input too: it finds the match of the test's own corner pattern and frames it.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid).copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    # the corner frame's colour is the cell just below-right of the 3x3 key\n    fc = g[3, 3]\n    key = g[0:3, 0:3]\n    found = None\n    for y in range(H - 2):\n        for x in range(W - 2):\n            if (y, x) == (0, 0):\n                continue\n            if np.array_equal(g[y:y+3, x:x+3], key):\n                found = (y, x)\n                break\n        if found:\n            break\n    if found is None:\n        emit(g, \"No second copy of the corner pattern found; grid unchanged.\")\n        return g\n    y, x = found\n    for yy in range(y - 1, y + 4):\n        for xx in range(x - 1, x + 4):\n            if 0 <= yy < H and 0 <= xx < W:\n                if yy in (y - 1, y + 3) or xx in (x - 1, x + 3):\n                    g[yy, xx] = fc\n    emit(g, f\"Found the corner 3x3 pattern again at rows {y}-{y+2}, cols {x}-{x+2}; drew a 5x5 frame of colour {fc} around it.\")\n    return g\n","candidate_tactics":{"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is the row y=3 and the column x=3, which enclose a 3×3 window at rows 0–2, columns 0–2."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Its 3×3 interior is left untouched."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Where the square goes.** Compare the interior of the new frame with the corner window:"}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"5969e53af0a241c8fe5b634f2926567dc215734021d0e73d2ad6f895e16595ea","task_sha256":"f8fbf21d4ba2143c5a1ef57d659ad9e9f1a70fddc36317c8ccb00237c7e82291","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
